course: dictionary
lesson: 1233
title: Bully: a word with a sting
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A bully is a person who hurts, frightens or picks on someone smaller or weaker again and again on purpose. The context gives a clue: «Bullies pick on children who are alone more often, so staying near friends and adults helps.» Picking on someone is what bullies do, and they choose people who are easier to hurt.
explain: Bully can also be a verb. To bully someone is to keep hurting, threatening, teasing or leaving them out on purpose. Notice how the word changes: bully, bullies, bullied, bullying. The pattern tells you whether it happens now, has happened, or is something a person does.
explain: «Mocking someone's religion or clothes, like a headscarf, is bullying.» Mocking means making fun of someone in an unkind way. Swap your meaning into the sentence: mocking someone is hurting them on purpose, so it is bullying. Look for clue words like mocking, threatening and leaving out.
explain: Remember that bullying is never the victim's fault. If it happens to you or someone you know, tell a trusted adult. Standing up for others is brave, and it helps stop the hurt.
fact: Bullying can be hitting, name-calling, spreading rumours, leaving people out, or online. Online bullying is called cyberbullying.
fact: «Making or sharing fake pictures of real people to mock them is bullying and can be against the law.»
fact: An upstander is someone who supports a person being bullied and tells an adult or reports it. This is not snitching.
fact: Bullying is never the victim's fault, and telling a trusted adult is always the right step.
fact: In stories, bullies often learn a lesson, or the hero finds the courage to stand up to them.
exercise: What does a bully do? | *Hurts or frightens someone smaller or weaker again and again on purpose | Makes new friends every week | Helps people who are lost | Plays games with everyone at break
exercise: Which form is the past tense of bully? | bullyer | bullies | bullying | *bullied
exercise: Someone supports a person being bullied and tells an adult. What is this person called? | A stranger | A bully | A snitch | *An upstander
test: Is mocking someone's clothes bullying? | No, it is just a joke | Only if it happens online | *Yes, it is bullying | Only if the person is an adult
test: You see someone being bullied at school. What is the best thing to do? | Laugh along with the group | Share a fake picture of them | Walk away and say nothing | *Tell a trusted adult
exam: What makes something bullying? | Helping a friend with homework | *Hurting or picking on someone again and again on purpose | Playing a game together at break | A single accident
